# Replaces the stock /usr/share/p11-kit/modules/opensc.module shipped in
# the Dakota base image, turning OpenSC's global PKCS#11 registration
# from an (incomplete) blacklist into a whitelist.
#
# Why: with a YubiKey plugged in at boot, `gpg --card-status` reports no
# card at all, and keeps reporting none until `systemctl restart pcscd`
# is run by hand. That is not a pcscd/udev startup race (an earlier
# diagnosis, and the reason bluefin-initial-setup once shipped an
# `ExecStartPre=udevadm settle` drop-in plus a udev rule restarting
# pcscd on every plug — both wrong, both since removed). It is
# contention for *exclusive* access to the card:
#
#   1. GnuPG's scdaemon always connects in SCARD_SHARE_EXCLUSIVE; it
#      needs that to unlock the card with the PIN without another
#      process injecting commands while it is unlocked.
#   2. This base image registers OpenSC as a *global* p11-kit module,
#      so any desktop daemon that touches NSS/PKCS#11 loads OpenSC,
#      which opens the card through pcscd.
#   3. Whoever gets there first during login wins. When that is a
#      desktop daemon via OpenSC, scdaemon's exclusive connect fails,
#      pcscd logs
#        pcscd: ../src/winscard.c:281:SCardConnect() Error Reader Exclusive
#      and GnuPG stays cardless until something drops the other client
#      — which is exactly what restarting pcscd does (it disconnects
#      every client at once).
#
# Upstream knows: the file this one replaces carries a FIXME describing
# precisely this, and prescribes the blacklist below as the workaround:
#
#   disable-in: goa-daemon gsd-smartcard evolution-source-registry \
#               evolution-calendar-factory gnome-shell
#
# That list is stale on current Dakota/Bluefin, where the user session
# also runs oo7-daemon (the gnome-keyring replacement),
# evolution-addressbook-factory, gnome-shell-calendar-server and
# gsconnect's gjs daemon — none of them listed. Every new desktop
# daemon reopens the hole. (Arch/NixOS escape this only by not
# installing and globally registering OpenSC in the first place;
# compare `p11-kit list-modules` there.)
#
# So: `enable-in` instead of `disable-in`. OpenSC's module is loaded
# only by the command-line tools below — programs that run when the
# user invokes them, never a daemon — and a desktop daemon added
# upstream tomorrow is blocked without editing any list.
#
# The cost: logging into a site or service with a PKCS#11 certificate
# (a PIV/CAC smartcard in Firefox/Chromium, say) stops working until
# that program is added to enable-in below. Not a loss on this machine:
# the YubiKey here serves the OpenPGP card (scdaemon, which does not
# use PKCS#11) and FIDO2/U2F (pam_u2f/libfido2, which does not either).
#
# Verify on a running host, after a reboot with the YubiKey already
# plugged in and *without* touching pcscd:
#
#   p11-kit list-modules     # must NOT list "module: opensc"
#   opensc-tool --atr        # must still read the card
#   gpg --card-status        # must still see the card
#
# (`p11-kit` itself is deliberately left out of enable-in, which is
# what makes the first command a meaningful check. `journalctl -t pcscd`
# showing "Error Reader Exclusive" again means something still holds
# the card.)
#
# Ported from bluefin-initial-setup, which installs the same content at
# runtime as /etc/pkcs11/modules/opensc.module
# (playbooks/templates/opensc-p11-kit.module.j2, tag
# yubikey-setup-pcscd) for Dakota hosts not using this image. Shipping
# it in /usr here leaves that /etc override free for the user; hosts
# that ran the playbook and now boot this image can delete their copy.
module: opensc-pkcs11.so
enable-in: opensc-tool opensc-explorer pkcs11-tool pkcs15-tool pkcs15-init p11tool
